Exercise 1 has no stdin file: hart is driven entirely from the command line.

Work in this directory, in place. That is what makes the two relative paths
below work -- the executable you built, and the basis set library:

  ln -s ../../build/hart hart      # once, if the link is not already here

  ./hart --job nh3 --basis def2-SVP --basis-dir ../../basis_sets --std-f nh3.hkl nh3.cif

About two seconds. (If you built into release/ rather than build/, point the
link there. If you would rather set TONTO_BASIS_SET_DIRECTORY once and for all,
do that and drop --basis-dir.)

Files here:

  nh3.cif   the starting (independent-atom) structure -- P 2_1 3, a = 5.1305 A
  nh3.hkl   88 reflections, free format: h k l F sigma
  nh3.out   the log from the run above, so you can compare

Notes on the options, since they are the whole of the input:

  --std-f       free-format h k l F sigma. Use --std-f2 if your file has F^2,
                --shelx-f / --shelx-f2 for the fixed-format SHELX layout.
  --basis       def2-SVP is also hart's default; written out on purpose, so
                that all three exercises visibly agree.
  --basis-dir   where the basis set files live. Only needed because we are
                running in place without TONTO_BASIS_SET_DIRECTORY set.

--dtol and --grid-accuracy used to be spelled out here and have been removed:
hart's defaults are already 0.01 and low, and a command line that only states
its defaults is longer without being clearer.

There is no --convergence option: hart does not expose the SCF energy
convergence, so the 0.001 that exercises 2 and 3 set in scfdata cannot be set
here. Flagged in WORKSHOP_PLAN.md -- it is a genuine gap between the two
programs, not an oversight in this deck.

Output: nh3.out (the log) and nh3.archive.cif (refined coordinates and ADPs
with esds).
